# Typography: use Kaizen's steps, at Kaizen's root

Every size decision in this application is a choice among Kaizen's `--text-*` steps. There is no
freehand `font-size`, and there is no global multiplier. The numbers below were measured from
Kaizen's own documentation site, not inferred from the token names.

## The steps

`<Text kind="body/{weight}/{step}">` resolves to a `--text-*` token. The names are sizes, not
opinions:

| step  | px | `display/` variant |
|-------|----|--------------------|
| `xs`  | 10 | 36 |
| `sm`  | 12 | 40 |
| `md`  | 14 | 44 |
| `lg`  | 16 | 50 |
| `xl`  | 18 | 56 |
| `2xl` | 24 | 64 |
| `3xl` | 32 | — |

Weights are `regular`, `semibold`, `bold`, `light`. `display/*` is its own scale and is for hero
text only — a page title is `2xl` or `3xl`, not `display`.

## What each step is for

Measured from Kaizen's site, which is the reference this product is supposed to look like:

- **`md` (14px)** — navigation, chrome, table cells, form controls. The workhorse. Kaizen's own
  nav is 14px, and ours inherits it from their `VerticalNav`, so this one is already correct by
  construction and must not be nudged.
- **`lg` (16px)** — body copy that someone actually reads: finding details, remediation text,
  panel descriptions. Prefer this over `md` whenever the text is a sentence rather than a label.
- **`xl` (18px)** — the lede: the paragraph that introduces a page or a section.
- **`2xl` / `3xl` (24 / 32px)** — section and page headings.
- **`sm` (12px)** — metadata and micro-labels: timestamps, counts, badge text, helper text.

Sections pair **`2xl` heading over `xl` lede**, which is what Kaizen's site does. Both halves
move together: an 18px lede under an 18px heading is the same size twice, and it reads as one
undifferentiated block rather than a heading and its introduction. `SectionHeading` in
`components/Feedback.tsx` owns this pairing for all 29 sections — change it there, not per view.

**`xs` (10px) is not used for text in this application.** It is below the floor for anything a
person is expected to read, and it is checked in CI. Kaizen spends it on badge chrome; we do not
have a case for it.

## Do not scale the root

`html` stays at the browser default. Raising it looks like it fixes "the text is too small" and
does not, because it multiplies the parts that were already right along with the parts that were
wrong. This was tried at 112.5% and made things worse in both directions at once: it pushed our
navigation to 15.75px where Kaizen's is 14px, while prose was still 13.5px against Kaizen's 18px.

If text is too small, the fix is that a call site picked the wrong step. Find it and change it.

A percentage on `html` is also how you take away a reader's own accessibility setting, and this
app is text-heavy enough that someone will have one.

## Five traps

These are recorded because each one cost real time, and each one disguises itself as a different
problem.

**Kaizen's preflight flattens semantic HTML, and rendered Markdown needs it back.** `base.css`
resets `h1..h6 { font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit }`, `ol, ul, menu { list-style: none }`
and `a { color: inherit; text-decoration: inherit }`. That is right for an application shell,
where a heading element is picked for semantics and sized by the component rendering it. Inside a
document it is wrong, and it presents as a broken Markdown parser: the standards README rendered
as a flat wall of 14px text with no bullets and no visible links, while the source was fine and
the renderer was emitting correct `<h2>`, `<ul>` and `<a>` all along. `.nvsf-prose` in `index.css`
restores all three, in Kaizen steps — and CI checks that it still does. Prose headings ladder
18 / 16 / 14, one level below the `2xl` `SectionHeading` above them.

**`body { font-size }` in `index.css` does nothing.** MUI's `<CssBaseline>` injects
`body { ...typography.body1 }` into `<head>` at runtime, after the bundled stylesheet, so it wins
on order every time. A dead rule sat in that file for the life of the project. Editing a
declaration that cannot take effect presents exactly as a stylesheet-order problem, which sends
you off to reorder imports and add override files — none of which can work either, because the
layer that wins is injected later than all of them.

**A size picked outside the scale is invisible to the scale's checks.** The 10px rule reads
`kind="..."` attributes, so a component that writes `fontSize: "var(--text-12)"` in an `sx` block
gets the same size the scale would have given it while nothing is watching. That is how a 12px
breadcrumb shipped after every `kind=` call site had been corrected. Size text with `kind=` on
`Text`; if a component genuinely needs a raw declaration — because it styles an anchor and a span
identically, which `Text` cannot do across a hover state — use the `--text-14` token and expect
CI to reject `--text-10` and `--text-12` there. Being secondary to a title is a job for
`--text-color-secondary`, not for a smaller step.

**Fixed pixel widths on anything that holds text.** A drawer, a card, a column declared as `248`
stays 248px while the text inside it grows with the reader's font size, so labels truncate inside
a container with room to spare. Widths that bound text are `rem`. `DRAWER_WIDTH_OPEN` is
`"15.5rem"`, not `248`, for this reason.

**Kaizen components that pin their own width.** `.nv-vertical-nav-root` ships with
`width: 240px`, which fought the Drawer containing it — two owners for one width, and the smaller
one clipped the labels. `index.css` releases it to `width: 100%`. Expect more of these; the fix is
to give the container sole ownership rather than to match the magic number.

## Checking your work

Do not judge type size from a screenshot — they get downscaled and everything looks fine. Measure
the computed styles in a real browser. `bin/check-frontend-typography.py` enforces the mechanical
rules above in CI, but it cannot tell you that a paragraph should have been `lg`.
